{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>The tenth of an ephah.</b> Even though it is in the category of poverty, it is sufficient for this, since there is from it the handful, an eating for the altar, and the remainder, an eating for the priest. This commandment is cherished in its time — they do not wait for him until he becomes rich and brings a lamb or a goat. And so with valuations: he gives a shekel immediately, and they do not wait for him until he becomes rich and brings fifty shekels." "<b>For it is a sin-offering.</b> It is fitting that a sin-offering require libations, so that the sinner not benefit — but why does it not require them? So that his offering not be adorned. But the sin-offering and the guilt-offering of a metzora, which do not come on account of sin, for he has already achieved atonement through the touching, and they come only to qualify the man — these do require libations, as our rabbis of blessed memory expounded in Sotah, in the chapter HaMekane."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:10:08.431627691Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}